---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Retrogrades', href: '/explore/retrogrades/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Retrogrades Explained"
  description="Retrograde motion is an optical effect of orbital geometry, not a planet reversing course. What Mercury, Venus, and Mars retrograde periods actually correspond to — minus the panic."
  path="/explore/retrogrades/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Retrogrades, explained</h1>
        <p class="lede">No planet has ever moved backwards. Here's what retrograde motion actually is, why astrologers still find it meaningful, and why Mercury retrograde is a better editor than it is a scapegoat.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.glassWave.src}
        alt={ocean.glassWave.alt}
        width={ocean.glassWave.width}
        height={ocean.glassWave.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The astronomy</h2>
      <p>
        Retrograde motion is a trick of perspective. Earth and the other planets orbit the Sun at
        different speeds, so when we overtake a slower outer planet — or an inner planet overtakes
        us — that planet appears, from our moving vantage point, to slow, stop, and drift backwards
        against the fixed stars before resuming. It's the same effect as passing a slower car on the
        highway: for a moment it seems to roll backwards relative to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in space reverses. The apparent path is real, though — it's observable, predictable,
        and has been tracked for millennia, which is why it entered astrological practice long
        before anyone could explain the geometry.
      </p>

      <h2>What astrologers make of it</h2>
      <p>
        The interpretive tradition is consistent: a retrograde planet's themes turn inward and
        backward. The functions that planet governs get reviewed, revised, reconsidered, and
        occasionally repaired. It's a season for the re- words rather than the pro- words.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a description of emphasis, not a prohibition. Life does not pause, contracts still
        get signed, and people still fall in love during retrogrades. What tends to be true is that
        work done in haste during these periods gets revisited.
      </p>

      <h2>The ones you'll actually notice</h2>
      <h3>Mercury — roughly three times a year, about three weeks each</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/mercury/">Mercury</a> governs communication, travel, contracts,
        and everyday logistics, and it retrogrades more often than any other planet — which is why
        it has the public profile. The traditional counsel is practical rather than superstitious:
        back things up, re-read before sending, confirm arrangements, and leave margin around travel
        and signatures.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest version: Mercury retrograde doesn't break your laptop. It's a period when
        things that were rushed, unclear, or half-agreed tend to surface — which feels like sabotage
        and is usually just an overdue reckoning. It's an excellent window for finishing, editing,
        and returning to something you abandoned.
      </p>
      <h3>Venus — about every 18 months, roughly six weeks</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/venus/">Venus</a> retrograde is rarer and, in my experience, more
        interesting. It reviews love, money, and values: old relationships resurface, aesthetic
        decisions get second-guessed, and the question of what you actually value versus what you
        were taught to want comes up for audit. Traditional practice advises against major cosmetic
        changes and new commitments made in a hurry — not because they're cursed, but because
        preferences formed during the review often shift once it ends.
      </p>
      <h3>Mars — about every two years, roughly two to three months</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/mars/">Mars</a> retrograde slows action and desire. Initiatives
        stall, anger turns inward, and the useful work is strategic rather than aggressive: refine
        the plan, address the frustration you've been suppressing, and hold the launch if you
        reasonably can.
      </p>

      <h3>The outer planets</h3>
      <p>
        Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto each retrograde for months every year — Saturn
        for roughly four and a half months, Neptune and Pluto for nearly half the year. Because
        they're so routine and so slow, they rarely register as day-to-day events. They're better
        understood as long interior review periods for that planet's themes.
      </p>

      <h2>A note on tone</h2>
      <p>
        Retrogrades have become astrology's most popular villain, and that's a shame — it's a
        genuinely useful timing concept flattened into an excuse. Nothing in the tradition says a
        retrograde removes your agency, and any forecast that tells you to hide until it passes is
        selling fear. Review is a skill. These are the seasons that reward it.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/current-sky/">what's retrograde right now</a>, or read
        <a href="/explore/transits/">how transits work</a> generally.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Wondering what a retrograde touches in your chart?"
    body="General sky-weather affects everyone loosely. What it activates in your chart specifically is a personal reading."
    buttonLabel="Monthly Personal Transits"
    buttonHref="/readings/monthly-transits/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
